Attack 1 — distribution math (rounding/sum drift): proportional 1500/750 split
of an 1600 override gives 1066.666.../533.333..., sum == 1600 to the cent
(existing test, confirmed). Probed further: single person → full override;
garbage-in negative+positive costs summing to 0 → falls into the zero-total
equal-split branch (500/500, sum=1000), no NaN/panic; zero override with
nonzero existing total → both persons become exactly 0 (0 is correctly a
"set" value, distinguishable from omitted since the pointer itself signals
presence); three persons with one at zero → the zero person gets 0 (0/total *
override), others split the total proportionally, sum matches exactly; a
1e308 override does not produce Inf (5e307 per person, finite). All probes
run via temp _test.go files added and removed, tree restored, no residue.

Attack 2 — engine consumption: `GetTotalHealthcareCost`/`GetMonthlyCostAt`
(models/healthcare.go, models/whatif.go) read HealthcarePersons[].
CurrentMonthlyCost / MonthlyHealthcare directly — confirmed by reading source,
and confirmed empirically by the worker's own
TestRunWithOverrides_HealthcareMonthlyCostMeasurablyChangesAnalysis, which
runs a real RunWithOverrides and shows FinalBalance differs (verified: test
passes in the full suite). For FRABenefit — the worker did NOT ship an
equivalent end-to-end engine test for social_security_fra_benefit/
spouse_fra_benefit (only struct-level Apply assertions exist; grep for
FRABenefit in mcpsvc/plan/overrides_test.go returns nothing). This is a real,
asymmetric test-coverage gap. I closed it myself with a probe
(TestProbe_FRABenefitOverrideChangesAnalysis, added then removed) using
RunWithOverrides on a base scenario with SocialSecurity configured: FRA=500
gives FinalBalance=1.678994e+06, FRA=5000 gives FinalBalance=1.860121e+06 —
materially different and directionally correct (higher benefit → higher final
balance). So the "silent no-op via income_sources" attack fails in practice,
even though the shipped test suite doesn't itself prove it. Flagging the gap
as a finding, not grounds for FAIL, since I independently verified the actual
behavior is correct.

